WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.100
Film Courage: So we’ve had a good two and
half, three hours here.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.040
Sometimes I’m in a time warp when I do these
interviews but we’ve gotten valuable information

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.040
from you Wendy.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.349
As we wrap up I would love to hear a success
story from you Wendy.

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:17.140
And if you can Wendy, please leave out any
identifying information (that’s kind of

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.410
a rule we have here).

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:24.150
Wendy Kram, Owner of Wendy’s LA4hire: So
let’s see I think there are couple.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:33.302
One would be from something that was quite
awhile ago that I had read a play by a writer

00:00:33.302 --> 00:00:37.510
and I was so enamored with the story.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:42.240
It was made and it was on CBS and it wound
up being called SALLY HEMINGS: AN AMERICAN

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.240
SCANDAL.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.910
And what I love about the story is that she
had written it and I had first found out about

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:52.770
it about seven or nine years before it got
made.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:01:00.590
And all I’ll say is that stars were aligned
and what happened was I came close to selling

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:06.060
it within the first two years that they had
the project it was under option (I think when

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.200
I was at Hearst Entertainment) and came close
but didn’t sell it.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:17.020
And then one day the writer and I read in
the trades that Merchant Ivory had sold a

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.439
movie called JEFFERSON in Paris with Nick
Nolte.

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:23.640
And this happens in the business.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.340
Invariably it happens to everyone.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:32.740
And right now I have two projects where there
are other competing projects in the marketplace

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.450
and we’re trying to get our’s done first.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:42.289
Anyway so the writer and I we were quite despondent
and it was like “Alright, life goes on.”

00:01:42.289 --> 00:01:45.899
And you put one foot in front of the other
and what’s the next project?

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:47.240
And you put you focus there.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.649
But I never forgot how much I loved that script.

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:59.279
And so Merchant Ivory movie was made and two
years later I felt like I would hear of opportunities

00:01:59.279 --> 00:02:08.179
in network television where they were interested
in doing period pieces and doing lots of period

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:13.890
films for network and so every couple of years
I would think “Maybe there’s a different

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.739
audience that we could get and reach with
TV?”

00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:23.269
And I would seek opportunities and I would
call up the writer and I would say “Is that

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:25.549
script still available?”

00:02:25.549 --> 00:02:27.049
And she’d say “Yes.”

00:02:27.049 --> 00:02:30.730
And one of those times seven years later she
said “Yes.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:38.731
As a matter of fact such-and-such actress
I was having lunch with her manager and we

00:02:38.731 --> 00:02:41.819
talked about it and she might be interested
in playing the role.”

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:48.000
So I would up calling CBS saying we don’t
have a commitment but we have preliminary

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.019
interest from so-and-so and she said “Well
send it over.”

00:02:52.019 --> 00:02:57.659
It was originally written as a 2-hour movie
and the network came back and said “You

00:02:57.659 --> 00:02:58.659
know what?

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:02.890
We think we might be interested in turning
it into a miniseries but instead of making

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:08.799
it so much about Thomas Jefferson we’d like
to make it much more Sally Hemings point of

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:09.799
view.”

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:10.810
So we did.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:17.780
We rolled up our sleeves, we spent a month
working with her preparing an outline to bring

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:23.450
to the network so we could shot what the end
of night one was that would ensure people

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:28.359
there was enough of a cliffhanger, that people
would come back for night 2 so we could justify

00:03:28.359 --> 00:03:31.620
yes this is enough material for a miniseries.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:41.800
The day we wind up going into pitch, two days
before, it wound up hitting the front page

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:48.640
of New York Times, Wall Street Journal, I
think Time Magazine that they had done tests

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:55.200
finally conclusively with Thomas Jefferson
and concluded from the DNA that he in fact

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.620
did have a long-term relationship with Sally
Hemings and that they did have five children

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:01.700
together.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:07.319
So all of our competitors were saying “Oh
my Gosh!

00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:12.450
You guys were so smart to be on it and in
the network two days after.”

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:19.239
Little did they know that this had started
about nine years earlier.

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:27.370
And as a lot of us have heard this expression
but it’s a very good one that ‘Success

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.210
is when preparedness meets opportunity.’

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:39.190
If I hadn’t fallen in love with the piece,
if the writer hadn’t written this wonderful

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:47.699
piece, if in fact I hadn’t continued every
two years to inquire and see if there was

00:04:47.699 --> 00:04:59.000
new opportunity and if we didn’t do our
homework…we spent a month preparing to go

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.000
in.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.360
I’ve seen very good writers who have blown
deals when they don’t prepare enough.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.789
Film Courage: You said something that caught
me ear and I want to follow-up.

00:05:08.789 --> 00:05:11.840
You’ve seen writers blow deals because they
weren’t prepared?

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:18.490
Are they not prepared to research the genre
that this network is dealing with?

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:25.009
Wendy: I would say it’s more with sometimes
if you have a script it can sell itself.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:31.280
But other times maybe you have a pitch, I
mean I’ve seen very, very established writers

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:37.960
go in a room and because they haven’t prepared
the pitch really well and it kind of meanders

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.130
and it could be a really good story.

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:48.990
But I’m a big advocate of preparing.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:56.500
A lot of people don’t know Whoopi Goldberg
when she started out she was an absolutely

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:06.180
brilliant comedian and when I saw her at a
charity event and she nailed it on stage and

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:12.240
there’s no question that there are certain
geniuses in comedy and are great at ad-libbing

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:22.890
and this and that but also for her and most
of the great comics they look so spontaneous.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:32.080
They work their butts off rehearsing and rehearsing
to be able to deliver so smoothly.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:39.599
So I do think it’s important if you’re
going to be pitching a project to be prepared.

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:45.400
That’s what we as producers had to do when
we were pitching the Sally Hemings piece and

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.090
it’s what I had to so all the time.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:52.800
And when I’m working with writers and we’re
going to go in and pitch, we have to do the

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:52.940
work.

